--- Defect B: real-view reconciliation (product pin 3c880d8; second, INDEPENDENT measurement, no rust_cli.py code
    used) ---
Our resolver, "product" ref only: TESTS = 576 (30 EXACT_RUST_CLI_DISPATCH + 5 HEURISTIC_RUST_CLI_DISPATCH_OUTER_ARM
+ 541 HEURISTIC_RUST_CLI_HARNESS_UNIQUE_METHOD), i.e. 576 distinct (test fn, resolved handler) edges.

Independent measurement 1 (raw call-site count, coordinator's own named shape):
  $ git grep -ozE '\.(ok|err)\(&\[\s*"[a-z][a-z0-9_-]*"' 3c880d8 -- 'tests/*.rs' 'tests/**/*.rs' | tr '\0' '\n' | wc -l
  -> 2420 raw call sites, across exactly 58 distinct test files (matches the ruling's own "58 test files" claim
  exactly: $ git grep -lzE ... | wc -l -> 58).

Independent measurement 2 (a DIFFERENT, non-tree-sitter Python script -- regex + `#[test]`-marker chunking, no
  import of govbridge.code.rust_cli at all -- counting DISTINCT (test-chunk, literal subcommand TOKEN) pairs,
  the closest apples-to-apples analogue of our own per-(test fn, handler) de-duplication):
  -> 636 distinct (test, subcommand-token) pairs across the same 58 files.

Reconciliation: 576 resolved edges vs 636 independently-counted (test, token) pairs -- same order of magnitude,
~9% lower, and the gap is explained, not hand-waved: (a) two different literal tokens occasionally dispatch to
the SAME final handler (e.g. a nested-subcommand alias), collapsing two (test,token) pairs into one (test,handler)
edge; (b) the crude `#[test]`-marker text-chunking independent method can attribute a helper call spanning past a
test's true closing brace to the wrong chunk (an approximation limit of the INDEPENDENT method, not of the
resolver being verified); (c) a token that fails to resolve against the real dispatch map at all (typo'd/removed
subcommand) contributes to the raw count but yields zero edges, by design (never guessed). No large,
unexplained gap.

--- Defect B: the zero-count "rust_harness_method_unresolved_by_reason" -- a SECOND, fully independent
    (raw git grep, no rust_cli.py) verification of WHY zero ambiguous harness-method names exist at the product
    pin ---
Our resolver found exactly 5 harness wrapper methods at 3c880d8, with bare names: gov, active_references_to, run,
ok, err (govbridge.code.rust_cli.find_impl_wrapper_methods).
Independent re-derivation, by raw grep alone (no Python resolver code): for EACH of those 5 names, search
`fn <name>(&self` across every .rs file at 3c880d8 and inspect by hand which definitions actually carry a
&[&str]/&[String]-shaped second parameter:
  fn ok(&self...)   -> 3 textual hits; only tests/certification/common.rs:195 has the &[&str] shape (the other
                        two, receipt.rs:178 and common.rs:112, are zero-arg `fn ok(&self) -> bool` -- a
                        DIFFERENT, unrelated method correctly excluded by the slice-parameter requirement)
  fn err(&self...)  -> 1 hit (tests/certification/common.rs:207), &[&str] shape
  fn run(&self...)  -> 1 hit (tests/certification/common.rs:160), &[&str] shape
  fn gov(&self...)  -> 1 hit (release/capability-baseline/verify-1/r1-preservation/heldout/kit.rs:237), a
                        DIFFERENT file entirely, &[&str] shape
  fn active_references_to(&self...) -> 1 hit (runtime/src/migrations/references.rs:494), &[String] shape
Every one of the 5 names has EXACTLY ONE real slice-parameter definition in the whole product-pinned corpus --
genuinely, structurally unambiguous. Confirmed independently, not merely asserted.

